Code Checker is a lightweight web-based coding playground for writing, running, and testing code in multiple languages directly in the browser.

The application includes a language selector that allows users to switch between Python, JavaScript, HTML, and several popular additional languages such as C, C++, Java, TypeScript, Ruby, PHP, Go, and C#. The editor updates automatically when a new language is chosen, and the output panel displays results or previews based on the selected language.

Python code runs in the browser using Pyodide, JavaScript executes directly in the page, and HTML is rendered as a live preview. For languages that cannot be executed in the browser without a compiler or runtime environment, the app provides a clear message explaining that the code needs a local toolchain or an external online compiler.

The project also includes useful session tools. Users can run code multiple times, track the number of completed runs, clear the editor, clear the session history, and export saved results as either a PDF or a TXT file. The PDF export creates a session report with all recorded code and output, while the TXT export saves the same information in a simple plain-text format.

The interface is clean, beginner-friendly, and responsive, making it easy to write code, switch languages, test snippets, troubleshoot errors, and save work for later. This project demonstrates how HTML, CSS, and JavaScript can be combined to build a practical coding tool for learning, experimentation, and quick code testing.
